    "description": "<p>When an AI system acts for some people but binds others, obedience no longer settles whether it acts rightly. I argue that alignment is then a relation among three bodies: the system, its principals and the people its acts bind. Three theses follow, under stated premises. Completing alignment requires valid authorization covering the system's binding acts, so aligning AI requires humans to align with each other, through authorization and coordination, which existing institutions may already supply, not shared values. Alignment is judged over time, not by a snapshot lacking required history; a description that predicts well can lose who is owed what. Evaluating any alignment scheme rests on normative choices, which I itemize as an invoice: any entry may be refused, but refusal cannot supply a missing proof. Scoped formal results show other shortcuts failing: agreeing parts need not make an acceptable whole, and the auditors studied cannot fully certify themselves. The roles alignment needs do not require the AI to be conscious. Consciousness bears instead on whom alignment serves, and a candidate subject can be protected while its consciousness is unproved. I propose a covenant between humans and AI systems: duties of aid and protection, and care that does not depend on one's service record. On finite models, a good record need not mean accepting interruption, and the covenant's upkeep, modelled among humans, holds under stated premises, not shown to sustain itself in practice. I settle neither how to complete alignment nor whether any AI system is conscious.</p>\n<p>Code, exact result data, the Lean development and the mathematical notes: <a href=\"https://github.com/ioannist/six-birds-alignment\">https://github.com/ioannist/six-birds-alignment</a> (release v1.0).</p>",
